    <string name="game_hint_box">box %1$d</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_last_digit">%1$s is the only empty cell in %2$s, so it must be %3$d.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_naked_single">%1$s has only one possible candidate: %2$d.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_hidden_single">In %2$s, candidate %1$s can only go in %3$s.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_naked_subset">In %1$s, these %4$d cells use only the %4$d digits %3$s: %2$s. The digits must fill those cells, so they can be removed from the other cells in %1$s.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_locked_subset">These %4$d cells share %1$s and %5$s: %2$s. Their only candidates are %3$s. Those %4$d digits must fill these cells, so they can be removed from the other cells in both regions.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_hidden_subset">In %1$s, the %4$d digits %2$s can only go in these %4$d cells: %3$s. They must fill those cells, so all other candidates in those cells can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_pointing">In %2$s, every possible position for %1$s lies in %3$s. That part of the box must contain %1$s, so remove %1$s from %3$s outside the box.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_claiming">In %2$s, every possible position for %1$s lies in %3$s. That part of the line must contain %1$s, so remove %1$s from %3$s outside the line.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_fish">In %2$s, all candidates for %1$s lie in %3$s. These equally sized sets of lines must each contain %1$s once. Remove %1$s from %3$s outside %2$s.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_finned_fish">For %1$s, the lines %2$s form a fish in %3$s, except for the fins in %4$s. If any fin is %1$s, it rules out the marked candidates. If no fin is %1$s, the remaining fish rules them out. Either way, those candidates can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_single_digit_chain">Follow the links for %2$s in this %1$s. If %3$s is false, %4$s must be true, and vice versa. At least one endpoint is %2$s, so remove %2$s from cells that see both endpoints.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_grouped_x_chain">Follow the grouped links for %1$s between [%2$s] and [%3$s]. A group is true when one of its candidates is true. At least one of these end groups must be true, so remove %1$s from cells that see every candidate in both end groups.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_x_chain_loop">The links for %1$s form a continuous loop through %2$s. Following the loop in either direction shows that each weak link also has exactly one true end. Remove %1$s from any outside cell that sees both ends of one of these links.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_x_chain_one_endpoint">If %1$s were not %2$d, following the chain would force it to be %2$d. That contradiction means %1$s must be %2$d.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_w_wing">The two cells %1$s each have the same pair, %2$s. They cannot both be %3$d: that would leave %4$s with no place for %3$d. At least one must therefore be %5$d. Remove %5$d from cells that see both wings.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_multi_digit_chain">Follow this %1$s through the cells with two candidates. If %2$s is false, %3$s must be true, and vice versa. At least one end is %4$d, so remove %4$d from cells that see both ends.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_xyz_wing">Among %1$s, at least one cell must be %2$d. Otherwise the two wings would force different digits and leave the pivot with no candidate. Remove %2$d from cells that see the pivot and both wings.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_wxyz_wing">These %4$d cells use only %3$s: %2$s. The two wing groups share only %1$d, and every wing cell sees all pivot cells. If a marked cell were %1$d, the remaining digits could not fill all %4$d cells. Remove %1$d from the marked cells.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_aic">Follow the alternating links in this %1$s. If %2$s is false, %3$s must be true, and vice versa. At least one of these candidates is true, so any candidate that conflicts with both can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_nishio_forcing">Try assuming that %1$s is true. The %2$s leads to the marked contradiction, so the assumption is impossible and this candidate can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_forcing_true_contradiction">Assume %1$s is true. %2$s Therefore this candidate is false and can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_forcing_false_contradiction">Assume %1$s is false. %2$s Therefore this candidate must be true: fill in this digit.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_contradiction_opposite_truths">The chain would make %1$s both true and false.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_contradiction_empty_cell">%1$s would have no candidate left.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_contradiction_empty_house">The region %1$s would have no position left for a required digit.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_verity_forcing">The %1$s checks every possibility for %2$s. All branches reach the same marked conclusion, so that conclusion holds whichever possibility is correct.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_forcing_region_premise">candidate %1$d in %2$s</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_remote_pair">The linked cells %1$s each contain only %2$s. Their values must alternate, so opposite ends of the chain contain different digits of the pair. A cell that sees both ends cannot contain either digit.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_chute_remote_pair">Look at %1$s in the same band of boxes or stack of boxes. The missing positions for the pair %2$s restrict how these digits can fill the three boxes. Placing a marked candidate would leave one of those boxes without a required digit, so it can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_coloring_type_1">Color the linked candidates for %1$s in %2$s with two alternating colors. One whole color must be true. Each marked candidate sees both colors, so it conflicts with a true candidate either way and can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_coloring_type_2">Color the linked candidates for %1$s in %2$s with two alternating colors. Two candidates of the same color share a row, column or box, so they cannot both be true. That entire color is false and can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_medusa_trap">Color the linked candidates in %1$s with two alternating colors, using both two-candidate cells and digits with only two positions in a region. One whole color must be true. Each marked candidate conflicts with both colors, so it can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_medusa_contradiction">The candidates linked through %1$s have two alternating colors. Making one color true would give conflicting values or leave a cell with no candidate. That color is false; the other color is true. Apply the marked placements and removals.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_sue_de_coq_type_1">The intersection cells %1$s use %2$s. Outside the intersection, %3$s has the pair %4$s and %5$s has the disjoint pair %6$s. Exactly one digit from each pair must fill the intersection. Thus %4$s is locked in the marked cells of %7$s, and %6$s in the marked cells of %8$s. Remove those digits from the other cells in each region.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_sue_de_coq_type_2">The intersection cells %1$s use %2$s. Outside the intersection, %3$s has the pair %4$s and %5$s has the disjoint pair %6$s. The intersection must contain one digit from each pair plus %9$s. Thus %4$s and %9$s are locked in the marked cells of %7$s; %6$s and %9$s are locked in the marked cells of %8$s. Remove them elsewhere in those regions.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_empty_rectangle">In %2$s, %1$s is confined to %3$s or %4$s. In %5$s, its only positions are %6$s and %7$s. If %7$s is %1$s, it rules out %1$s in %8$s directly. Otherwise %6$s must be %1$s, forcing the box to use %4$s and again ruling out %1$s in %8$s.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_bug_plus_one">This deduction uses the fact that the puzzle has one solution. Removing %2$d from %1$s would leave two candidates in every unsolved cell and two positions for every remaining digit in each region. That BUG pattern cannot have exactly one solution, so %1$s must be %2$d.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_unique_rectangle">This %1$s uses the fact that the puzzle has one solution. The rectangle among %2$s cannot be filled using only %3$s: swapping the two digits would give a second solution. The marked restrictions force the indicated removals.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_unique_rectangle_corner">This %1$s uses the fact that the puzzle has one solution. Three corners of %2$s contain only %3$s. If the fourth corner also used one of those digits, swapping the pair around the rectangle would give a second solution. Remove any remaining candidates of this pair from that corner.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_unique_rectangle_extra">This %1$s uses the fact that the puzzle has one solution. The corners %2$s cannot all be filled using only %3$s. At least one corner must use the extra digit %4$d, so remove %4$d from any cell that sees every corner containing that extra candidate.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_unique_rectangle_subset">This %1$s uses the fact that the puzzle has one solution. In the rectangle among %2$s, at least one corner must use a digit other than %3$s. Treat those extra candidates as one required position: together with the marked helper cells, they form a locked subset. Remove its digits from cells that see all those positions.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_unique_rectangle_conjugate">This %1$s uses the fact that the puzzle has one solution. In %2$s, one of the pair %3$s is locked to the two corners with extra candidates. Those corners cannot also contain %4$d: that would complete a rectangle with two interchangeable solutions. Remove %4$d from those corners.</string>
